  <tr><td>Childless stepmother v. stepson</td><td>Arguable as &ldquo;mother&rdquo;; strongest when widowed and childless</td><td>Yes — explanation to Section 20 includes childless stepmother</td><td>Yes — &ldquo;parent&rdquo; includes step-parent</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Stepmother with own children</td><td>Weak; expected to claim from own sons/husband</td><td>Outside the Section 20 explanation</td><td>Yes, if a senior citizen; Tribunal apportions among children</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>The child&rsquo;s side: practical routes in blended families</h2>
<p>Where a minor lives with the mother and stepfather, and the natural father is alive, the clean claim is the child&rsquo;s petition under Section 144 BNSS against the natural father — his remarriage, or the mother&rsquo;s, does not dilute the obligation. Where the natural father has died, counsel should look to the estate: HAMA Section 22 makes heirs liable to maintain the deceased&rsquo;s dependants from inherited property, and a minor child is a dependant of the first order. The DV Act adds a further layer where the mother is an aggrieved person: monetary relief under Section 20 of that Act expressly includes maintenance &ldquo;for the aggrieved person as well as her children&rdquo;, a phrase wide enough to fund the actual household in which the children live.</p>

<h2>The stepmother&rsquo;s side: building the claim</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Choose the forum first.</strong> For an elderly stepmother, the Senior Citizens Act Tribunal is usually the remedy of choice: definitions are explicit, procedure is summary, and the ceiling-free maintenance order is enforceable with teeth.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Plead the dependency facts.</strong> Widowhood, absence of her own children, lack of independent income, and the stepson&rsquo;s means — the equities the provisions are built around.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Under HAMA, invoke the explanation to Section 20.</strong> For a childless stepmother, the statutory text does the heavy lifting; quantification then follows ordinary Section 23 factors.</div>
</div>

<h2>Points both sides should watch</h2>
<div class="check">
  <p>The step-relationship must be real in substance: courts examine whether the claimant was actually part of the respondent&rsquo;s family fold, not merely connected by a late remarriage.</p>
  <p>Multiple obligors can coexist — natural children, stepchildren, a living spouse. Apportionment arguments should be raised at the first hearing, not in execution.</p>
  <p>Orders under one statute do not bar another forum, but amounts are adjusted; disclose every pending or decided claim to avoid the double-recovery objection.</p>
  <p>For conduct after 1 July 2024, cite the BNSS provision (Section 144) rather than Section 125 CrPC, while noting that the substantive principles carry over.</p>
</div>
<div class="note">This explainer states general legal principles for information only. Step-relationship maintenance claims are fact-intensive, and specific advice should be taken on the actual family structure involved.</div>
